WEBVTT

00:00:00.048 --> 00:00:04.423
The kind of machine that answers your ques
tions weighs more than a tonne and draws

00:00:04.498 --> 00:00:06.443
as much power as a hundred homes.

00:00:06.541 --> 00:00:08.741
Ask it why your cat
sounds like a carburettor,

00:00:08.791 --> 00:00:10.616
and watch what that monster does.

00:00:10.616 --> 00:00:11.766
A few words.

00:00:11.766 --> 00:00:12.591
A pause.

00:00:12.591 --> 00:00:13.718
A few more words.

00:00:13.793 --> 00:00:17.693
It is the most powerful computer
most of us will ever touch,

00:00:17.743 --> 00:00:20.202
and yet it writes like
someone pecking at a keyboard.

00:00:20.343 --> 00:00:21.462
So why does it stutter?

00:00:21.509 --> 00:00:24.259
The obvious guess is
that it is thinking hard,

00:00:24.309 --> 00:00:27.221
that every single word takes
a staggering amount of maths.

00:00:27.290 --> 00:00:30.915
Keep that guess in mind,
because we are going to test it.

00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:35.415
The real answer explains nearly everything
about the buildings behind artificial

00:00:35.465 --> 00:00:36.257
intelligence.

00:00:36.297 --> 00:00:39.922
To see it, let's follow a single
word of your question on its trip.

00:00:39.972 --> 00:00:40.822
The word cat.

00:00:40.862 --> 00:00:44.906
Cat leaves your phone as a radio wave
and reaches a mast down the street.

00:00:45.001 --> 00:00:46.776
From there it travels as light,

00:00:46.851 --> 00:00:49.280
trapped inside a strand of
glass thinner than a hair.

00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:52.096
But how do you keep light
inside a bent piece of glass?

00:00:52.169 --> 00:00:57.019
In eighteen forty two, a Swiss physicist
called Daniel Colladon showed the trick

00:00:57.069 --> 00:00:58.091
with a jet of water.

00:00:58.189 --> 00:01:02.489
Light that hits the inside of the
jet at a shallow angle cannot escape,

00:01:02.539 --> 00:01:04.470
so it follows the water as it curves.

00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:07.095
Glass fibre does exactly the same thing,

00:01:07.145 --> 00:01:09.525
bouncing the light off
its walls over and over.

00:01:09.601 --> 00:01:14.726
In twenty seventeen, a team at Heriot Watt
University in Edinburgh actually filmed

00:01:14.776 --> 00:01:14.955
it.

00:01:14.995 --> 00:01:18.470
Their camera caught a pulse of
light racing around a coil of fibre,

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:19.481
loop after loop.

00:01:19.521 --> 00:01:23.754
In glass, light covers about two hundred
thousand kilometres every second.

00:01:23.828 --> 00:01:28.342
A thousand kilometres of cable costs
cat about five thousandths of a second.

00:01:28.456 --> 00:01:30.331
So the trip is not the slow part.

00:01:30.406 --> 00:01:31.297
Hold on to that.

00:01:31.337 --> 00:01:33.862
The light ends in a
warehouse of computers,

00:01:33.912 --> 00:01:36.712
like the one built for OpenAI in Abilene,

00:01:36.762 --> 00:01:37.377
Texas.

00:01:37.417 --> 00:01:39.942
And there cat runs into its first problem.

00:01:39.992 --> 00:01:41.717
The chips inside cannot read.

00:01:41.765 --> 00:01:43.690
They only understand numbers,

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:46.453
so your question has to be
chopped into pieces first.

00:01:46.526 --> 00:01:49.205
The way it gets chopped
comes from an unlikely place.

00:01:49.278 --> 00:01:51.128
In February nineteen ninety four,

00:01:51.128 --> 00:01:56.353
a programmer in Colorado Springs called
Philip Gage wrote a magazine article about

00:01:56.428 --> 00:01:57.362
shrinking files.

00:01:57.402 --> 00:01:59.127
His idea was simple.

00:01:59.227 --> 00:02:02.102
Find the pair of characters
that appears most often,

00:02:02.152 --> 00:02:03.733
and replace it with a new symbol.

00:02:03.806 --> 00:02:05.456
Then do it again, and again,

00:02:05.506 --> 00:02:06.720
until the file is small.

00:02:06.793 --> 00:02:11.518
Two decades later, Rico Sennrich and his
colleagues borrowed that trick to split

00:02:11.568 --> 00:02:13.333
words for translation software.

00:02:13.406 --> 00:02:15.881
Common words end up as single pieces,

00:02:15.956 --> 00:02:19.008
rare ones as fragments, and
every piece gets a number.

00:02:19.077 --> 00:02:22.202
ChatGPT's tokenizer works the same way.

00:02:22.302 --> 00:02:26.624
In it, cat with a space in front is
number nine thousand and fifty nine.

00:02:26.697 --> 00:02:28.322
That number is a key.

00:02:28.397 --> 00:02:30.647
It unlocks a long list of other numbers,

00:02:30.647 --> 00:02:32.725
a kind of address for
the meaning of the word.

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:34.986
On a map, two numbers
say where something is.

00:02:35.081 --> 00:02:37.531
Here every token gets thousands of them,

00:02:37.581 --> 00:02:40.555
so it sits somewhere in a space
with thousands of directions.

00:02:40.649 --> 00:02:44.349
Training arranges that space
so that cat ends up near dog,

00:02:44.399 --> 00:02:46.123
and a long way from carburettor.

00:02:46.171 --> 00:02:47.671
But cat is not done yet.

00:02:47.746 --> 00:02:48.875
Now comes the climb.

00:02:48.926 --> 00:02:53.099
OpenAI does not say how big
the models behind ChatGPT are.

00:02:53.147 --> 00:02:57.222
But in twenty twenty five it published
an open model built the same way,

00:02:57.272 --> 00:02:58.059
with the blueprint.

00:02:58.099 --> 00:03:01.624
That model is made of about a hundred
and seventeen billion numbers,

00:03:01.674 --> 00:03:02.522
called weights.

00:03:02.562 --> 00:03:04.112
Nobody typed them in.

00:03:04.187 --> 00:03:06.337
Training tuned them, step by step,

00:03:06.437 --> 00:03:08.203
on trillions of tokens of text.

00:03:08.276 --> 00:03:10.326
They are stacked in thirty six layers,

00:03:10.376 --> 00:03:11.926
like the floors of a tower,

00:03:11.976 --> 00:03:13.835
and cat has to climb every floor.

00:03:13.954 --> 00:03:18.929
On each floor, cat looks back at every wor
d before it and decides which ones matter.

00:03:19.004 --> 00:03:20.555
That step is called attention.

00:03:20.656 --> 00:03:23.819
It is how the word it gets
linked to the thing it refers to.

00:03:23.892 --> 00:03:27.067
Then cat visits a crowd of
smaller networks on that floor,

00:03:27.117 --> 00:03:28.029
called experts.

00:03:28.069 --> 00:03:29.919
But it does not visit all of them.

00:03:29.919 --> 00:03:33.802
A small router sends it to just four
out of a hundred and twenty eight.

00:03:33.922 --> 00:03:37.707
So on the whole climb, cat only
touches about five billion weights.

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:41.055
Each touch is a multiplication
and an addition,

00:03:41.105 --> 00:03:44.982
so that is roughly ten billion
operations for a single new word.

00:03:45.050 --> 00:03:47.825
What comes out of the top
floor is not a sentence.

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:50.378
It is a score for every
possible next piece.

00:03:50.427 --> 00:03:53.277
The system picks one,
usually a likely one,

00:03:53.327 --> 00:03:56.203
with a little randomness so it
does not sound like a robot.

00:03:56.276 --> 00:03:59.201
And then the whole climb starts
again from the ground floor,

00:03:59.251 --> 00:04:00.185
for the next piece.

00:04:00.225 --> 00:04:02.650
A long answer means hundreds of climbs,

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:03.596
one after another.

00:04:03.636 --> 00:04:06.111
Ten billion operations for every word.

00:04:06.161 --> 00:04:07.261
So that must be the stutter,

00:04:07.311 --> 00:04:07.921
right?

00:04:07.961 --> 00:04:08.591
The maths?

00:04:08.631 --> 00:04:09.656
Actually, no.

00:04:09.706 --> 00:04:10.856
That guess is wrong.

00:04:10.906 --> 00:04:12.630
The arithmetic is the easy part.

00:04:12.702 --> 00:04:14.627
To see what the real problem is,

00:04:14.677 --> 00:04:16.170
picture the chip as a factory.

00:04:16.219 --> 00:04:19.119
The workers who do the
multiplying sit at desks.

00:04:19.169 --> 00:04:20.694
The weights do not live with them.

00:04:20.766 --> 00:04:22.766
They live in a warehouse next door,

00:04:22.816 --> 00:04:24.691
and for every new piece of the answer,

00:04:24.741 --> 00:04:25.877
they have to be trucked in.

00:04:25.996 --> 00:04:27.746
All of the weights that cat touches,

00:04:27.796 --> 00:04:28.946
every time.

00:04:28.996 --> 00:04:32.042
For that open model, the
load is about five gigabytes.

00:04:32.141 --> 00:04:34.416
Now take a Blackwell chip from NVIDIA,

00:04:34.466 --> 00:04:36.139
the kind in that Texas warehouse.

00:04:36.212 --> 00:04:40.960
Its workers can finish ten billion operati
ons in about four millionths of a second.

00:04:41.033 --> 00:04:44.133
Its memory can deliver about
eight terabytes a second,

00:04:44.183 --> 00:04:45.333
which sounds enormous.

00:04:45.402 --> 00:04:46.902
But watch the race.

00:04:47.002 --> 00:04:51.527
At that speed, the five gigabyte delivery
takes more than six hundred millionths of

00:04:51.477 --> 00:04:52.125
a second.

00:04:52.165 --> 00:04:54.848
The truck takes over a hundred
times longer than the work.

00:04:54.942 --> 00:04:56.867
So when the chip writes for just you,

00:04:56.942 --> 00:05:00.139
its workers sit idle more than
ninety nine percent of the time.

00:05:00.212 --> 00:05:01.787
That is the pause you see.

00:05:01.837 --> 00:05:03.851
Engineers call it being memory bound.

00:05:03.924 --> 00:05:06.667
And none of this surprised
the people who saw it coming.

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:11.440
In nineteen ninety four, two computer
scientists at the University of Virginia,

00:05:11.515 --> 00:05:13.590
William Wulf and Sally McKee,

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:15.670
wrote a short paper with a blunt title.

00:05:15.763 --> 00:05:16.970
Hitting the Memory Wall.

00:05:17.019 --> 00:05:20.319
Processors were getting faster
much more quickly than memory,

00:05:20.369 --> 00:05:22.880
they pointed out, and
the gap kept widening.

00:05:23.024 --> 00:05:24.974
Sooner or later, they warned,

00:05:25.049 --> 00:05:28.824
every program would end up waiting
for memory instead of computing.

00:05:28.899 --> 00:05:30.923
Cat's climb is exactly that case.

00:05:31.017 --> 00:05:33.942
They were right, and the
wall is not just about time.

00:05:34.017 --> 00:05:35.017
It is about energy.

00:05:35.066 --> 00:05:39.221
Twenty years later, the Stanford
engineer Mark Horowitz put numbers on it.

00:05:39.315 --> 00:05:43.590
Fetching a number from a memory chip cost
about a hundred and seventy times more

00:05:43.640 --> 00:05:45.984
energy than multiplying two numbers.

00:05:46.053 --> 00:05:47.853
The distance is the cost.

00:05:47.928 --> 00:05:50.028
Every bit has to be pushed down a wire,

00:05:50.078 --> 00:05:52.213
and the longer the
wire, the more it burns.

00:05:52.307 --> 00:05:55.707
So the question for everyone
building these machines became:

00:05:55.782 --> 00:05:57.397
how do you stop moving so much?

00:05:57.445 --> 00:05:59.658
The first answer was
to move the warehouse.

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:04.608
At AMD, a team led by Bryan Black had been
working on it since two thousand eight.

00:06:04.656 --> 00:06:06.806
Their problem was graphics memory.

00:06:06.881 --> 00:06:10.389
Its power use simply would not
scale to what the next chips needed.

00:06:10.508 --> 00:06:13.333
So instead of spreading
memory chips around the board,

00:06:13.383 --> 00:06:14.826
they stacked them into towers.

00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:17.800
Tiny wires run straight
down through the silicon,

00:06:17.850 --> 00:06:20.203
connecting every floor
of the tower at once.

00:06:20.276 --> 00:06:23.151
Then they set the towers
right beside the processor,

00:06:23.226 --> 00:06:24.683
on the same slab of silicon.

00:06:24.756 --> 00:06:29.731
In June twenty fifteen, AMD's Fiji was
the first graphics chip to ship with this

00:06:29.781 --> 00:06:30.625
stacked memory.

00:06:30.665 --> 00:06:33.240
Today it is called high bandwidth memory,

00:06:33.290 --> 00:06:35.883
and every Blackwell chip
carries its own towers of it.

00:06:35.934 --> 00:06:40.064
The road from warehouse to factory is
now as short as anyone can make it.

00:06:40.133 --> 00:06:41.583
But there is a catch.

00:06:41.658 --> 00:06:45.583
Even on that short road, the truck still
takes hundreds of times longer than the

00:06:45.633 --> 00:06:46.006
work.

00:06:46.046 --> 00:06:49.984
So the second answer was to stop
sending the truck for just one person.

00:06:50.057 --> 00:06:53.099
Every conversation uses
exactly the same weights.

00:06:53.193 --> 00:06:56.218
So the system gathers many
conversations together,

00:06:56.268 --> 00:06:59.926
and a single delivery carries cat
along with everyone else's words.

00:06:59.973 --> 00:07:02.973
The workers who would nap for
you stay busy for a crowd.

00:07:03.048 --> 00:07:04.320
This is called batching.

00:07:04.414 --> 00:07:06.914
Except batching has a problem of its own.

00:07:06.989 --> 00:07:09.035
Every conversation needs a scratchpad.

00:07:09.129 --> 00:07:11.354
For every word you send or receive,

00:07:11.404 --> 00:07:14.354
the model keeps the notes
it made during attention,

00:07:14.404 --> 00:07:15.819
so it never has to redo them.

00:07:15.892 --> 00:07:18.867
Those notes grow with every
word and every person,

00:07:18.917 --> 00:07:20.192
and they fill the memory up.

00:07:20.265 --> 00:07:24.515
In twenty twenty three, Woosuk Kwon and
his colleagues at Berkeley measured how

00:07:24.565 --> 00:07:25.068
badly.

00:07:25.108 --> 00:07:27.208
In the serving systems of the time,

00:07:27.283 --> 00:07:31.051
as little as a fifth of that
scratchpad memory held actual notes.

00:07:31.099 --> 00:07:33.349
The rest was reserved just in case,

00:07:33.399 --> 00:07:35.744
or lost in gaps between conversations.

00:07:35.792 --> 00:07:39.567
Their fix came from an old
idea in operating systems.

00:07:39.642 --> 00:07:43.445
Cut memory into small pages and
hand them out only when needed.

00:07:43.493 --> 00:07:45.243
With almost nothing wasted,

00:07:45.293 --> 00:07:48.650
the same chips could handle two
to four times as many requests.

00:07:48.702 --> 00:07:53.327
A year earlier, Tri Dao and his colleagues
had applied the same lesson inside

00:07:53.352 --> 00:07:54.312
attention itself.

00:07:54.352 --> 00:07:56.627
Their method, called FlashAttention,

00:07:56.677 --> 00:07:59.702
keeps the work in a tiny
fast memory on the chip,

00:07:59.777 --> 00:08:01.771
so less has to travel back and forth.

00:08:01.844 --> 00:08:04.374
The third answer was to
make the cargo lighter.

00:08:04.449 --> 00:08:08.849
In that open model, most weights are
stored in just over four bits each,

00:08:08.899 --> 00:08:10.304
instead of the usual sixteen.

00:08:10.444 --> 00:08:13.469
Smaller numbers mean a
lighter truck on every trip,

00:08:13.519 --> 00:08:15.445
and the whole model fits on a single chip.

00:08:15.493 --> 00:08:18.143
But the biggest models
do not fit on one chip,

00:08:18.193 --> 00:08:20.394
and a busy service
needs thousands of them.

00:08:20.489 --> 00:08:22.314
So the chips are wired together,

00:08:22.364 --> 00:08:24.683
and here the fight against
distance gets physical.

00:08:24.734 --> 00:08:27.009
This is NVIDIA's rack for Blackwell,

00:08:27.009 --> 00:08:30.102
a cabinet taller than a person
that weighs more than a tonne.

00:08:30.174 --> 00:08:32.324
It holds seventy two of those chips,

00:08:32.399 --> 00:08:35.306
along with thirty six Grace
processors to manage them.

00:08:35.358 --> 00:08:38.008
Behind them runs a spine of copper cables,

00:08:38.083 --> 00:08:39.958
more than three kilometres of it,

00:08:40.033 --> 00:08:41.707
linking every chip to every other.

00:08:41.780 --> 00:08:44.105
NVIDIA says the rack acts like a single,

00:08:44.180 --> 00:08:45.355
massive GPU.

00:08:45.428 --> 00:08:48.703
A model can be cut into
slices and spread across it,

00:08:48.753 --> 00:08:51.776
so each chip only fetches
its own slice of cat's climb.

00:08:51.870 --> 00:08:53.632
Where does all that effort go in the end?

00:08:53.705 --> 00:08:55.205
It turns into heat.

00:08:55.280 --> 00:08:58.730
One of these racks draws about
a hundred and twenty kilowatts,

00:08:58.780 --> 00:09:00.992
about as much as a hundred American homes.

00:09:01.068 --> 00:09:02.843
Air cannot carry that away,

00:09:02.918 --> 00:09:05.941
so liquid runs through metal
plates pressed onto every chip.

00:09:05.989 --> 00:09:07.689
The heat has to go somewhere,

00:09:07.739 --> 00:09:10.784
and many sites dump it into
the sky through cooling towers.

00:09:10.857 --> 00:09:13.882
A tower lets a small part
of the water evaporate,

00:09:13.932 --> 00:09:16.245
and the evaporation pulls
heat out of the rest.

00:09:16.343 --> 00:09:18.592
That is where the water
in the headlines goes.

00:09:18.690 --> 00:09:21.165
Days after ChatGPT launched,

00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:25.739
its chief executive Sam Altman wrote
that the compute costs were eye-watering.

00:09:25.833 --> 00:09:27.733
How much does cat's trip actually cost,

00:09:27.783 --> 00:09:28.161
then?

00:09:28.201 --> 00:09:33.126
In twenty twenty five, he put an average
query at about a third of a watt hour,

00:09:33.201 --> 00:09:35.616
about what an oven uses in a second or so.

00:09:35.689 --> 00:09:37.514
Tiny, for one question.

00:09:37.564 --> 00:09:41.013
But ChatGPT gets around two and
a half billion prompts a day.

00:09:41.086 --> 00:09:44.661
Together, that is a steady load
of about thirty five megawatts,

00:09:44.711 --> 00:09:47.050
the power of roughly
thirty thousand homes.

00:09:47.127 --> 00:09:49.077
And yet that is a single service,

00:09:49.127 --> 00:09:50.806
in an industry that is still building.

00:09:50.895 --> 00:09:52.384
Which brings us back to cat.

00:09:52.507 --> 00:09:54.157
Light carried it through glass,

00:09:54.157 --> 00:09:56.821
a trick from a jet of water
in eighteen forty two.

00:09:56.894 --> 00:10:01.294
A file compression idea from a nineteen
ninety four magazine turned it into a

00:10:01.294 --> 00:10:01.718
number.

00:10:01.758 --> 00:10:04.483
It climbed a tower of weights
that had to be trucked in,

00:10:04.558 --> 00:10:07.210
again and again, for
every piece of the answer.

00:10:07.326 --> 00:10:09.151
Stacked memory shortened the road,

00:10:09.201 --> 00:10:10.651
batching shared the truck,

00:10:10.701 --> 00:10:12.523
and smaller numbers lightened the load.

00:10:12.596 --> 00:10:17.496
Every one of those inventions fights the
same wall that two scientists described in

00:10:17.546 --> 00:10:18.533
nineteen ninety four.

00:10:18.573 --> 00:10:20.736
Moving a number costs more than using it.

00:10:20.830 --> 00:10:23.805
So the next time an answer
appears a few words at a time,

00:10:23.880 --> 00:10:24.758
watch the rhythm.

00:10:24.798 --> 00:10:27.136
That little stutter is
not the machine thinking.

00:10:27.237 --> 00:10:29.013
It is the machine waiting for the truck.
